# Rigor core overlay — supplemental core signatures.
#
# --- OpenStruct#method_missing (issue #1456) ---
#
# rbs 4.2 ships no `ostruct` signatures (since Ruby 4.0 `ostruct` is a bundled
# gem, not a default one), and the gem ships no `sig/`. The only `OpenStruct`
# declaration in the environment is json's add-on reopening in
# `stdlib/json/0/json.rbs` (`json_create` / `as_json` / `to_json`), so the
# class read as fully declared and `OpenStruct.new(name: "x").name` reported
# `call.undefined-method`.
#
# Upstream: ostruct 0.6.3 `lib/ostruct.rb` defines each field as a
# per-instance singleton method (`new_ostruct_member!` through
# `define_singleton_method!`) and answers the rest through
# `private def method_missing(mid, *args)`, which this file declares. It
# defines no `respond_to_missing?` of its own (Kernel's answers, and the
# fields are real singleton methods), so none is declared here.
#
# The per-field accessors cannot be declared on the class at all, so
# `Rigor::Analysis::CheckRules::CORE_OVERLAY_OPEN_RECEIVERS` names
# `OpenStruct` as an open receiver: an RBS `method_missing` alone does not
# stop the undefined-method rule. That list is what quiets `rigor check`;
# this file supplies the declaration the maintainer decision asked for.
#
# The declaration sits on a module the class includes, as `process.rbs`
# does, so a project `sig/` that declares `OpenStruct#method_missing` itself
# overrides it instead of raising `DuplicatedMethodDefinitionError`.
#
# Maintainer decision on #1456 (2026-10-16, option c): this narrow overlay.
# The general rule for a class declared only by another library's partial
# reopening is #1978.
module RigorCoreOverlay
  module OpenStructMissing
    private def method_missing: (Symbol mid, *untyped args) -> untyped
  end
end

class OpenStruct
  include RigorCoreOverlay::OpenStructMissing
end
